\section1 Player

To implement the \c Player we first need a player class.\br
Because we need an \c Enemy class later, we will implement the \c Entity first.\br

\section3 Entity

\c entities/Entity.qml
\code
import QtQuick
import Felgo

EntityBase {
    id: entity

    property int row // of tile
    property int column // of tile
    property string imgSrc // has to be squared for right image sizing
    property int imgSize

    Image {
        x: -entity.imgSize/ 2
        y: -entity.imgSize / 3 * 2
        width: entity.imgSize
        height: entity.imgSize
        smooth: false
        source: entity.imgSrc
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Player

From that we can create the \c Player. \br
\c entities/Player.qml
\quotefile Player.qml
\br

\section3 Additional Functions in the Tilemap

Now we need a \c PlayerManager for the \c Player.
For that we need to add a few more functions to the \c Tilemap. \br
With these functions you can calculate the actual position and set the occupation of a \c Tile. \br
\c tilemap/Tilemap.qml
\code
Item {
    id: tilemap

    ...

    // calculate x and y of tile center
    function tileIndexToPosition(row,column) {
        let y = (row +0.5) / rows * height;
        let x = (column + 0.5) / columns * width;
        return Qt.point(x,y);
    }

    function changeEntityTileOccupation(currentRow,currentColumn,newRow,newColumn) {
        tiles[currentRow][currentColumn].occupied = false;
        tiles[newRow][newColumn].occupied = true;
    }

    function setEntityTileOccupation(row,column) {
        tiles[row][column].occupied = true;
    }

    function removeEntity(row,column) {
        if(tiles[row] !== undefined && tiles[row][column] !== undefined) {
            tiles[row][column].occupied = false;
        }
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 PlayerManager

Then to manage the \c Player we add the \c PlayerManager.
So far it only spawns the \c Player. \br
\c entities/PlayerManager.qml
\code
import QtQuick
import Felgo

EntityManager {
    id: playerManager
    property variant tilemap
    property variant player

    function spawnPlayer(row,column) {
        const playerPostion = tilemap.tileIndexToPosition(row,column);
        tilemap.setEntityTileOccupation(row,column);

        if(player === undefined) {
            var newEntityProperties = {
                row: row,
                column: column,
                imgSize: tilemap.tileSize,
                x: playerPostion.x,
                y: playerPostion.y
            };

            playerManager.createEntityFromUrlWithProperties(
                        Qt.resolvedUrl("Player.qml"),
                        newEntityProperties);
            player = getLastAddedEntity();
        } else {
            player.row = row;
            player.column = column;
            player.imgSize = tilemap.tileSize;
            player.x = playerPostion.x;
            player.y = playerPostion.y;
        }
        entityContainer.changeVisualEntityOrder();
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 EntityContainer

Now we need to create an \c EntityContainer, which acts as parent for the entities and handles the order.\br
\c entities/EntityContainer.qml
\quotefile EntityContainer.qml
\br

\section3 Integration

Both need to be added to the \c DungeonScene. \br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
...
import "../entities"
...
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    Item {
        ...

        Tilemap {
            ...

            EntityContainer {
                id: entityContainer
            }
        }

        PlayerManager {
            id: playerManager
            entityContainer: entityContainer
            tilemap: tilemap
        }
    }

    function createMap() {
        ...
        playerManager.spawnPlayer(playerTileIndex.row,playerTileIndex.column);
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 How it looks

\image Player_On_Tilemap.png
